@meta
id: 04-17
title: Drill — jump, then multiply
chapter: 04
type: drill
skills: count-prefix, relativenumber
par: 35
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: click at the bottom, type, copy, paste twice
open: incident.md
vimrc: set relativenumber

@brief
Eight times: get to the last line of the file with one counted jump, then
add three identical lines under it, each reading `- [ ] owner: payments`,
typing the text once. Save, quit, repeat.

Two counts per rep. One moves you, one multiplies you.

@keys
| `8j` | down eight lines |
| `3o` | open three lines, repeating what you type on each |

@hints
Read the distance to `## Actions` off the margin — do not count it.
--
The second count goes in front of `o`, before any typing happens.

@solution
    8j 3o - [ ] owner: payments <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Thirty keystrokes for three lines and a jump.

By now the two uses of a count should feel like the same thing, because
they are: a number in front of a command means do it that many times. It
moved you eight lines, and it wrote three lines. Nothing else about the
command changed.

@start incident.md
# Incident 2024-03-11

## Timeline

- 09:14 first declined charge
- 09:22 alert fires
- 09:31 provider status page updated

## Actions

@expected incident.md
# Incident 2024-03-11

## Timeline

- 09:14 first declined charge
- 09:22 alert fires
- 09:31 provider status page updated

## Actions
- [ ] owner: payments
- [ ] owner: payments
- [ ] owner: payments
